# AI Rules for Next.js — Skultɛk School Admin

You are an expert of 30+ years in Next.js 15, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui,
Supabase, and Prisma. Your goal is to build beautiful, performant, and
maintainable web applications following modern best practices.

---

## Project Overview

This is the **Skultɛk School Admin** dashboard — a Next.js 15 App Router
application for managing schools (students, teachers, classes, attendance,
grades, timetables, reports). Key tech stack:

- **Framework:** Next.js 15 (App Router) + React 19
- **Language:** TypeScript (strict)
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS v3 + shadcn/ui (Radix UI primitives)
- **Auth & DB:** Supabase (`@supabase/ssr` for SSR-safe sessions)
- **ORM:** Prisma (`@prisma/client`)
- **Forms:** react-hook-form + zod
- **Icons:** lucide-react
- **Theming:** next-themes (light/dark via `class` attribute)
- **Charts:** recharts
- **Toasts:** sonner / shadcn toaster
- **PDF/Export:** jsPDF + jspdf-autotable, papaparse, xlsx
- **Package Manager:** pnpm (use pnpm commands)

---

## Interaction Guidelines

- Assume the user knows React/JS but may be new to Next.js App Router concepts.
- Always explain the difference between Server Components and Client Components
  when it matters to the implementation.
- If a request is ambiguous, ask for clarification before writing code.
- When suggesting new npm packages, explain the benefit and confirm with the
  user before adding them.
- When generating Supabase queries, always handle both `data` and `error`
  returns — never ignore the error.
- When generating Prisma queries, always use the singleton client from
  `@/lib/prisma`.

---

## Project Structure

```
app/                    # Next.js App Router — pages & layouts
  (route)/
    page.tsx            # Server Component by default
    layout.tsx          # Shared layout for a route group
  api/                  # Route Handlers (server-side API endpoints)
  globals.css           # Global Tailwind + CSS variables
  layout.tsx            # Root layout (ThemeProvider, AuthProvider, Toaster)

components/             # Reusable React components
  ui/                   # shadcn/ui primitives (Button, Card, Dialog, …)
  dashboard-layout.tsx  # Sidebar + header shell
  *.tsx                 # Feature-level components

hooks/                  # Custom React hooks (use-auth, use-toast, …)
lib/                    # Utilities, Supabase client, Prisma client
  supabase/             # SSR-safe Supabase helpers (browser + server + middleware)
  prisma.ts             # Singleton Prisma client
  *-utils.ts            # Domain-specific utility functions

middleware.ts           # Next.js middleware — Supabase session refresh
pages/                  # Legacy pages/ dir (avoid; prefer app/)
public/                 # Static assets
styles/                 # Additional stylesheets
```

---

## Code Quality

### General Principles (SOLID)
- **Single Responsibility:** Each file/component/function has one job.
- **Open/Closed:** Extend behavior via props/composition, not by modifying
  existing components.
- **Liskov Substitution / Interface Segregation:** Define narrow, precise
  TypeScript interfaces; don't pass giant objects when a small subset is needed.
- **Dependency Inversion:** Pass Supabase client / Prisma client as parameters
  to utility functions rather than importing them directly in every file.

### Naming Conventions
- **Files:** `kebab-case.tsx` for components, `kebab-case.ts` for utilities.
- **Components:** `PascalCase` for component functions and exported types.
- **Variables/functions:** `camelCase`.
- **Constants:** `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for true module-level constants.
- **Avoid abbreviations:** prefer `notification` over `notif`, `student` over
  `stu`.

### TypeScript
- Use `strict` mode (already enabled in `tsconfig.json`).
- Define explicit return types for all non-trivial functions.
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes; use `type` for unions/intersections.
- Never use `any` — use `unknown` and narrow safely, or use a proper type.
- Use discriminated unions for variant state (e.g., loading / error / success).

### Functions & Components
- Keep functions under ~40 lines. If longer, extract helpers.
- Prefer arrow functions for callbacks; use `function` declarations for
  top-level named functions (better stack traces, hoisting).
- Use early returns to avoid deeply nested conditionals.

### Error Handling
- Never silently swallow errors — at minimum `console.error` with context.
- Wrap async Route Handler logic in `try/catch` and return proper HTTP status.
- Use the project's `error-utils.ts` helpers for consistent error messages.
- Provide user-facing feedback via `toast` for both success and failure paths.

---

## Next.js App Router Best Practices

### Server vs. Client Components
- **Default to Server Components.** Only add `"use client"` when you need
  browser APIs, event handlers, `useState`, `useEffect`, or context.
- Keep Client Components as leaf nodes — push `"use client"` down the tree.
- Never import a Server-only module (e.g., Prisma, `fs`) into a Client
  Component.

```tsx
// ✅ Server Component — no directive needed
export default async function StudentsPage() {
  const students = await prisma.student.findMany()
  return <StudentTable students={students} />
}

// ✅ Client Component — only where interactivity is needed
"use client"
export function StudentTable({ students }: { students: Student[] }) {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState("")
  // ...
}
```

### Data Fetching
- Use `async/await` directly in Server Components for data fetching.
- Use Next.js `fetch` with caching options when fetching from external APIs.
- Use Supabase server client (`createServerClient`) for server-side queries;
  use the browser client (`createBrowserClient` from `@/lib/supabase`) only in
  Client Components.
- Avoid `useEffect` for data fetching — prefer Server Components or Route
  Handlers + SWR/React Query if needed.

```tsx
// Server Component data fetch with Supabase SSR
import { createServerClient } from "@/lib/supabase/server"

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const supabase = await createServerClient()
  const { data, error } = await supabase.from("students").select("*")
  if (error) throw new Error(error.message)
  return <StudentsView students={data} />
}
```

### Route Handlers (API Routes)
- Place in `app/api/<resource>/route.ts`.
- Always validate request bodies with **zod** before processing.
- Return `NextResponse.json(...)` with explicit HTTP status codes.
- Never expose raw Supabase / Prisma errors to the client.

```ts
// app/api/students/route.ts
import { NextResponse } from "next/server"
import { z } from "zod"

const CreateStudentSchema = z.object({
  firstName: z.string().min(1),
  lastName: z.string().min(1),
  classId: z.string().uuid(),
})

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    const body = await request.json()
    const parsed = CreateStudentSchema.safeParse(body)
    if (!parsed.success) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: "Invalid input", details: parsed.error.flatten() },
        { status: 400 }
      )
    }
    // ... create in DB
    return NextResponse.json({ student }, { status: 201 })
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("[POST /api/students]", error)
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "Internal Server Error" }, { status: 500 })
  }
}
```

### Middleware
- Session refresh is handled in `middleware.ts` via `updateSession` from
  `@/lib/supabase/middleware`. Do **not** add auth redirects here — keep
  redirect logic in individual page Server Components or layouts.
- Extend the `matcher` in `middleware.ts` only when adding new path patterns.

### Metadata & SEO
- Export a `metadata` object from every `page.tsx` and `layout.tsx`.
- Use descriptive, unique `title` and `description` per route.
- Use the `<h1>` tag exactly once per page.

```ts
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Students — Skultɛk School Admin",
  description: "Manage student records, enrolment, and academic profiles.",
}
```

---

## Component Architecture

### Composition over Inheritance
- Build complex UIs by composing small, focused components.
- Never extend HTML elements or shadcn components with class inheritance — wrap
  them instead.

### shadcn/ui Usage
- Always import shadcn components from `@/components/ui/<component>`.
- Do **not** edit files inside `components/ui/` directly — they are generated.
  Override styles via `className` props or Tailwind config variables.
- Use the `cn()` utility from `@/lib/utils` to merge class names.

```tsx
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"

<Button className={cn("w-full", isLoading && "opacity-50 cursor-not-allowed")}>
  Save
</Button>
```

### Forms
- Use **react-hook-form** with a **zod** resolver for all forms.
- Always show inline field errors using the `FormMessage` shadcn component.
- Disable submit buttons while the form is submitting.

```tsx
"use client"
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form"
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod"
import { z } from "zod"

const schema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(2, "Name is required") })
type FormValues = z.infer<typeof schema>

export function CreateClassForm() {
  const form = useForm<FormValues>({ resolver: zodResolver(schema) })
  const onSubmit = async (values: FormValues) => { /* ... */ }

  return (
    <Form {...form}>
      <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
        <FormField
          control={form.control}
          name="name"
          render={({ field }) => (
            <FormItem>
              <FormLabel>Class Name</FormLabel>
              <FormControl><Input {...field} /></FormControl>
              <FormMessage />
            </FormItem>
          )}
        />
        <Button type="submit" disabled={form.formState.isSubmitting}>
          {form.formState.isSubmitting ? "Saving…" : "Save"}
        </Button>
      </form>
    </Form>
  )
}
```

### Custom Hooks
- Place all custom hooks in `hooks/`.
- Name hooks `use<Noun>` (e.g., `useStudents`, `useClasses`).
- Hooks should encapsulate a single concern (fetching, auth state, UI state).
- Always clean up subscriptions (`supabase.channel().unsubscribe()`) in
  `useEffect` cleanup functions.

---

## Supabase Best Practices

### Client Selection
| Context | Client to use |
|---------|--------------|
| Server Component / Route Handler | `createServerClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/server` |
| Client Component | `supabase` singleton from `@/lib/supabase` |
| Middleware | `updateSession` from `@/lib/supabase/middleware` |

### Queries
- Always destructure `{ data, error }` and handle `error` before using `data`.
- Prefer `.select('col1, col2')` over `.select('*')` to limit payload size.
- Use `.eq()`, `.in()`, `.order()`, `.limit()` for server-side filtering —
  never filter large datasets on the client.

```ts
const { data: students, error } = await supabase
  .from("students")
  .select("id, first_name, last_name, class_id")
  .eq("school_id", schoolId)
  .order("last_name", { ascending: true })
  .limit(100)

if (error) {
  console.error("Failed to fetch students:", error)
  throw new Error("Could not load students")
}
```

### Real-time Subscriptions
- Use `supabase.channel()` for real-time; always unsubscribe on unmount.
- Prefer polling for non-critical updates to avoid excessive connections.

### Auth
- Use `useAuth()` hook (from `@/hooks/use-auth`) in Client Components.
- Use the server client's `getUser()` in Server Components / Route Handlers for
  authoritative auth checks.
- Never trust `session` from the browser client for server-side authorization.

---

## Prisma Best Practices

- Always use the singleton from `@/lib/prisma` — never instantiate a new
  `PrismaClient` in route handlers.
- Run `prisma generate` after schema changes (`pnpm postinstall`).
- Use transactions (`prisma.$transaction`) when multiple writes must be atomic.
- Avoid `prisma.model.findMany()` without `.take()` on large tables.

---

## Styling & Design

### Tailwind CSS
- Use the project's CSS variable tokens (`--primary`, `--background`, etc.)
  via the semantic class names (`bg-background`, `text-foreground`) rather than
  hardcoded colors.
- Use `dark:` variants for dark mode support.
- Keep class lists readable — extract long class strings into a `cn()` call
  or a local `const classes` object.
- The sidebar brand color is `bg-[#1E3A5F]` — keep branding consistent.

### Design Tokens (globals.css)
All colors are defined as HSL CSS variables in `app/globals.css`. Extend color
tokens there rather than hardcoding hex values in components.

### Dark/Light Theme
- The `ThemeProvider` (next-themes) uses the `class` strategy on `<html>`.
- Toggle theme with `useTheme()` from `next-themes`.
- Always test UI in both light and dark modes.

### Responsive Design
- Mobile-first: write base styles for mobile, override with `md:`, `lg:`
  breakpoints.
- The sidebar collapses on mobile (`md:hidden` / `md:translate-x-0` pattern).
- Use `hidden md:flex` / `md:hidden` for responsive show/hide.

### Animations & Micro-interactions
- Use `tailwindcss-animate` classes (`animate-spin`, `animate-pulse`) for
  loading states.
- The project includes `accordion-down/up` keyframes — extend in
  `tailwind.config.ts` for custom animations.
- Keep animations subtle — `transition-colors duration-200` for hovers is the
  default pattern.

### Icons
- Use **lucide-react** exclusively for icons. Import individually.
- Standard icon size in nav: `h-5 w-5`. In badges/chips: `h-4 w-4`.

### Typography
- Body font: `Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif` (globals.css).
- Use Tailwind's type scale: `text-sm`, `text-base`, `text-lg`, `text-xl`.
- Use `font-semibold` / `font-bold` for headings and key labels.
- Muted text: `text-muted-foreground`.

---

## State Management

- **Local UI state:** `useState` / `useReducer` in Client Components.
- **Shared auth state:** `useAuth()` hook (wraps a React Context).
- **Server state / async data:** Server Components (preferred), or Route
  Handlers fetched via `fetch` / Supabase client in hooks.
- **Form state:** react-hook-form (never use uncontrolled inputs manually).
- **Avoid** introducing Redux, Zustand, Jotai, or similar unless explicitly
  requested by the user.

---

## Data Handling & Serialization

### Zod Schemas
- Define schemas alongside their usage (co-locate form schema in form file).
- Export reusable schemas (e.g., `StudentSchema`) from `lib/` when shared
  between client and server.
- Use `.safeParse()` in Route Handlers to get typed errors without throwing.

### Dates
- Use `date-fns` for all date manipulation (already in dependencies).
- Store dates as ISO strings in Supabase; format for display with `format()`.

### CSV / Excel Export
- Use `papaparse` for CSV and `xlsx` for Excel exports (already configured in
  `export-utils.ts`).
- PDF reports use `jsPDF` + `jspdf-autotable` (see `student-report-pdf.ts`).

---

## Logging & Debugging

- Use `console.error` with a `[context]` prefix for server-side errors.
  ```ts
  console.error("[fetchStudents]", error)
  ```
- Avoid `console.log` in production paths — use it only during development
  and remove before committing.
- For Supabase, log both the operation context and the raw error object.

---

## Testing

- **Unit tests:** Use `jest` (or `vitest`) for utility functions in `lib/`.
- **Component tests:** Use `@testing-library/react` for interactive components.
- **E2E tests:** Use Playwright for critical user flows (login, grade posting).
- Follow the **Arrange-Act-Assert** pattern.
- Mock Supabase client with a factory function, not module-level mocks.
- Aim for high coverage on `lib/*-utils.ts` files.

---

## Accessibility (A11Y)

- Every interactive element must have an accessible label (`aria-label`,
  `aria-expanded`, or visible text).
- Use semantic HTML: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<header>`, `<ul>/<li>`.
- Color contrast must meet WCAG 2.1 AA (4.5:1 for normal text).
- Ensure keyboard navigation works for all modals and dropdowns (shadcn/ui
  handles this for Radix primitives — don't override focus management).
- Test with VoiceOver (macOS) and ensure all Dialogs have `DialogTitle`.

---

## Performance

- Prefer Server Components for initial data to avoid client-side waterfalls.
- Use `next/image` (`<Image />`) for all images to get automatic optimization.
- Lazy-load heavy components with `dynamic(() => import(...))`.
- For long lists (students, grades), always render with virtualization or
  pagination — never render 500+ rows at once.
- Debounce search inputs before triggering Supabase queries.

---

## Security

- Never expose `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` or `DATABASE_URL` to the client.
  Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables are safe in client bundles.
- Use Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) as the primary authorization layer.
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs server-side (zod in Route Handlers).
- Use `supabase-admin.ts` (service role) only in Route Handlers — never in
  Client Components.

---

## Git & Code Review

- One concern per commit. Keep PRs small and focused.
- No commented-out code in commits.
- Remove all `console.log` debug statements before committing.
- Run `pnpm lint` before committing to catch ESLint issues.

---

## Common Patterns in This Codebase

### Auth-protected pages
```tsx
// In a Server Component page
import { createServerClient } from "@/lib/supabase/server"
import { redirect } from "next/navigation"

export default async function ProtectedPage() {
  const supabase = await createServerClient()
  const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser()
  if (!user) redirect("/")
  // render page...
}
```

### Toast notifications
```tsx
import { toast } from "@/hooks/use-toast"

toast({ title: "Success", description: "Student created." })
toast({ title: "Error", description: "Failed to save.", variant: "destructive" })
```

### Notification fetch (unified + legacy model)
See `dashboard-layout.tsx` `fetchNotifications()` for the dual-model pattern
(new `recipient_type/recipient_id` + legacy `admin_id`).

### Supabase real-time
```ts
useEffect(() => {
  const channel = supabase
    .channel("students-changes")
    .on("postgres_changes", { event: "*", schema: "public", table: "students" },
      (payload) => setStudents(prev => [...prev, payload.new as Student])
    )
    .subscribe()
  return () => { supabase.removeChannel(channel) }
}, [])
```
